::GEO_U06_L08_GP01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L08_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which tool supports a coordinate proof that two sides are perpendicular? {
=A. Slope#Negative reciprocal slopes support perpendicular lines.
~B. Volume formula#Volume does not prove perpendicular sides.
~C. Scale factor cubed#This applies to volume scaling, not perpendicularity.
~D. Cross-section sketch only#A sketch alone is not enough coordinate proof.
}

::GEO_U06_L08_GP02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L08_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.9<br><br>Question:<br>What is the radius of (x - 1)&sup2; + (y + 2)&sup2; = 36? {
~A. 36#The right side is r&sup2;, not r.
=B. 6#The radius is sqrt(36)=6.
~C. 1#This is part of the center, not the radius.
~D. 2#This is part of the center, not the radius.
}

::GEO_U06_L08_GP03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L08_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.14<br><br>Question:<br>Which unit should a volume answer use? {
~A. meters#Meters are linear units.
~B. square meters#Square meters measure area.
=C. cubic meters#Volume uses cubic units.
~D. degrees#Degrees measure angles.
}

::GEO_U06_L08_GP04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L08_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A solid is enlarged by scale factor 3. How does volume change? {
~A. 3 times#That is the linear effect.
~B. 6 times#This doubles the scale factor, not the volume rule.
~C. 9 times#That is the area effect.
=D. 27 times#Volume changes by k&sup3;, so 3&sup3;=27.
}

::GEO_U06_L08_GP05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L08_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.11<br><br>Question:<br>A slice of a rectangular prism parallel to one face forms what type of cross-section? {
=A. A rectangle congruent or similar to that face#A parallel slice of a rectangular prism matches the face shape.
~B. Always a circle#Rectangular prisms do not have circular faces.
~C. Always a triangle#A parallel slice to a rectangular face is rectangular.
~D. A sphere#A cross-section is two-dimensional, not a three-dimensional sphere.
}
